eople  confirmed positive we were definitely  nervous  we were afraid of getting phone calls in  the middle of the night hearing about  yet another new case  this time the command center reacted  quickly  tracking all possible contacts and home  quarantining over 4  300 people they also transferred  negative test patients to other  hospitals  to decrease the workload on staff after  14 days with no new confirmed cases  all hospital employees had to do mass  testing to convince the public that the  hospital  is safe in 44 days the outbreak was  contained  with only 21 cases and just one death  transparency and honesty is very  important  and you have to give the people the  reason why you want to have  a very stringent contation and very  stringent home quarantine  and this and the evidence we have to  show the evidence that it is very  very useful to prevent the future  pandemic  chance that no countries can fight alone  i  would like to re-emphasize the important  thing is the prudent surveillance  we have to keep eye on the possible  outbreak in all  the countries to prevent its spread out  now taurian hospital is back to relative  normality  the stars experience reminded government  and its people  not to let history repeat itself a  lesson not only for taiwan  but for the world in its approach to  tackling this fast spreading virus  martin reese is co-founder of the center  for the study of existential risk  an interdisciplinary research group  focused on studying and developing  strategies  to risks that could threaten humanity  martin it's good to have you on the show  first of all  let's start with the pandemic before we  talk about talk about  existential risk rather i can barely  even say the word um we just saw about  taiwan and how its management of the  pandemic has been shaped by sars  but at the same time other countries  have had their own experiences with  infectious diseases  and yet they've struggled in their  management of the pandemic why do we see  that it differs so much from country to  country  well i think we in the uk had prepared  for an influenza pandemic  but of course we didn't have the  experience of sars and of course  uh coronaviruses need different  preparations they need protective  equipment for everyone  and it's hard to develop a vaccine etc  so we in the uk were badly prepared as  uh i think in some respects were uh  other european countries  um and i think the uh taiwan  had a government that was more trusted  perhaps than our uk government was by  its people and that do you helped  that we're worse long-term planners than  we believe we are potentially or do we  just lack imagination or is it a  combination of both  um i think we are worse long-term  planners um  obviously we can't predict when a  pandemic would strike but no one could  say it was unlikely  uh given that we had sars and mers  in the past and i think there are some  lessons we can learn obviously  one is that it pays to build in a bit of  slack in the system or resilience i mean  for instance i know  in germany you had more empty beds in  your  intensive care wards than we normally  have we try and keep them full  all the time which means you've got no  spare capacity and  also um we shouldn't depend so much on  supply chains  for manufacturing because if you have  just one long chain and one link breaks  that screws up a lot of manufacturing  so i think we've learned that it's more  important to have resilience  than maximum efficiency okay so perhaps  we're not the best long-term planners  for things like pandemics even though  actually they're quite likely  what about for truly existential threats  what you're focused on that could be  anything i gather from  a more serious illness to an asteroid  potential asteroid strike  what does that mean for planning for for  those threats  well of course you can't they're just  extremes and i should say that uh  with herman astronomer i'm not kept  awake at night by  asteroids 