imize or reduce inequalities which  have been actually accentuated  by the pandemic and people like myself  for instance can go on working  from home etc but many people can't and  those who are in  cramped apartments with no gardens etc  have had a really tough time so i think  the experience of the pandemic has  accentuated  the effect of inequalities which many of  us think are far too great anyway  and i hope to be pressured to reduce  them  martin reece is co-founder of the center  for the study of existential risk i'll  get that word right eventually martin  thank you so much for joining us thank  you very much  well our science correspondent derek  williams is also thinking about the  future  here he is with a question on the future  of mrna technology  we came up with vaccine for covid in  under a year what do developments like  that mean for the future of healthcare  covet 19 vaccines represent an  absolutely  immense achievement in the midst of a  raging pandemic  governments and pharma companies and  scientists  came together to produce vaccines at  lightning speed to fuel the biggest  vaccination drive in history which is  now progressing  at an astonishing pace um additionally  the coronavirus has triggered an  absolute  avalanche of research that's already  having ripple effects on areas  far far beyond health care  sars kovi 2 has promoted even closer  cooperation  between science and industry while  at the same time revealing glaring  weaknesses  in health care systems it's also exposed  um the many many ways that it's possible  for governments  to mess up communications with the  public despite all that we've gotten  wrong  though i remain positive overall and  and i think that history will look back  on these years where  where so many lost so much  as as the first time that humanity came  together  on a global scale to fight a problem  that threatens us all covet 19  has sparked advances in a really wide  range of fields  but i want to focus on on